KG42.register("rules-04212d5503ba1f86d34c39d8",{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R004","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","name":"Price-signal coordination","process_stage":"coordination","market_meaning":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges. Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period. Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function coordination.price_signal under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply","coordination.price_signal"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R004-E001","source":"bidding.demand","target":"coordination.price_signal","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R004-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R004-E002","source":"bidding.supply","target":"coordination.price_signal","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R004-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. | Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. | Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g02-E14"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm","symbol":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k}","meaning":"Bilateral trading price, described as the generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}, at negotiation iteration k; it is updated from received counterproposal quantities.","kind":"operational","domain":"Not explicitly specified in the supplied source; occurs as a price variable in the algebraic update Eq. (6b).","unit":null,"indices":["n\\in\\Omega","m\\in\\omega_n","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e11","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R004-x-p-nm","symbol":"p_{nm}^{k}","meaning":"Signed bilateral traded power from agent n to agent m at negotiation iteration k; positive when agent n is selling and negative when buying.","kind":"operational","domain":"Signed real power with role-dependent restrictions in Eq. (6a): p_{nm}\\ge0 if n\\in\\Omega_g, p_{nm}\\le0 if n\\in\\Omega_c, and \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n if n\\in\\Omega_p.","unit":null,"indices":["n\\in\\Omega","m\\in\\omega_n","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-rho","symbol":"\\rho","meaning":"Penalty factor in the consensus-ADMM coordination update; the source states \\rho>0.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":">0"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-pri","symbol":"\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}}","meaning":"Primal global feasibility tolerance in the stopping criterion associated with the decentralized negotiation.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-dual","symbol":"\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}}","meaning":"Dual global feasibility tolerance in the stopping criterion associated with the decentralized negotiation.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-gamma-nm","symbol":"\\gamma_{nm}","meaning":"Network charge associated with power trade p_{nm}; when used as a network charge it is provided a priori by the system operator.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["n\\in\\Omega","m\\in\\omega_n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e21","v141-b-g01-e24"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals.","expression":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^{k}-\\rho\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)/2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm","v141-c-R004-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-theta-rho"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g01-e18"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals.","expression":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^{k}-\\rho\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)/2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm","v141-c-R004-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-theta-rho"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g01-e18"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e04","v141-b-g01-e21"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E14"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E14"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","expression":"\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|r_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}2},\\quad \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|s_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}2};\\quad r_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)^2,\\quad s_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}-p_{nm}^{k}\\right)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-pri","v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-dual"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (1e)-(1g)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"Note that the deﬁnition of partnership set ωn only inform on agent n’s possible trades but does not ","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"As proven in Appendix A, when no network charges are applied, i.e. all \\gamma_{nm}=0, trading prices \\lambda_{nm} are uniform and equal to the pool market price. The system operator does not intervene in solving (5) as it only provides network charges \\gamma_{nm} a priori. Hence, network charges are provided in a transparent manner before negotiations such that agents can anticipate on the over costs brought by the use of the power system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"rm on. n∈Ω n∈Ω the with, respectively, primal and dual local residuals ies 2 rk+1n = pk+1nm + pk+1mn","equation_id":"Eq. (7)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Global stopping criteria associated to (6) are such as \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|r_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}2} and \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|s_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}2} (7); with, respectively, primal and dual local residuals r_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)^2 (8a); s_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}-p_{nm}^{k}\\right)^2 (8b); Parameters \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}} and \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}} denotes primal and dual global feasibility tolerances, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based et- on the revenue adequacy and the c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Both electrical distance and zonal cost allocation policies depend on grid characteristics, supposed time independent. Their unit fees can be adapted to grid’s status (e.g. between day and night). As any exogenous approach the proposed allocation policies may not ensure efficiency of the P2P market, as pointed in [16] in the case of transmission rights. Even though local marginal prices seem effective, they may be largely rejected for their opacity by P2P market participants anxious for transparency. To define the unit fees, as well as zones, the system operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based on the revenue adequacy and the congestion occurrence rate of the last period. This type of historical data analysis is a common practice. For example the French transmission operator, RTE, publishes its transmission tariffs (or TURPE for Tarif d’Utilisation du Réseau Public d’Electricité) based on this type of analysis. More details on this method can be found in the “Study on tariff design for distribution systems” prepared for the European Commission. Alternatively, zones can follow administrative delimitation such as states."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p02-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"gy g , pp y a network charge component. This network charge component is not only to recover all net","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In contrast, the bilateral contracts considered here have a component that is based on energy generation costs, supplemented by a network charge component. This network charge component is not only to recover all network costs but also other costs e.g. operational, taxes and policy-related costs. Hence, network constraints are not forced directly but rather accommodated through these network charges. Another important benefit of this approach is that market participants have knowledge of network charges prior to the negotiation process. Contrary to a classical economic dispatch, this transparency on network charges enables agents to anticipate on what it will cost them to trade on the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"y pn pn p for bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g). It is essential to notice that contrary ","equation_id":"Eq. (1b) and Eq. (1h)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"It is essential to notice that contrary to [16] dual variables of nodal power balances (1i), noted η_i, do not include the energy generation price but only prices to derive nodal energy prices. Note that congestion rights originate from (1h)’s dual variable. In our case, energy generation prices are given by the dual variables for trade reciprocity (1b), denoted Λ = (λ_nm)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 4, JULY 2019 fn to be closed, proper, and convex is","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"local residuals. In this context it would be interesting to use a secured mechanism, as does [15] fo","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"When the goal is to obtain a P2P market with allocation of grid-related costs it is possible to use network charges as in (4). Contrary to preference prices chosen by agents, parameters \\gamma_{nm} are provided by the system operator a priori when used as network charges. As mentioned in Section I, remember that, in this paper, a cost allocation policy refers to the way costs are divided between P2P market participants. The proposed cost allocation policies will define how network charges are estimated."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e24","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"whA. Total Fees m. The money paid (resp. received) by agent n for buying if t from (resp. selling to","equation_id":"Eq. (9)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The money paid (resp. received) by agent n for buying from (resp. selling to) agent m is given by the perceived price \\mu_n=\\lambda_{nm}-\\gamma_{nm}, as shown in Appendix A. Network charges represent exogenous costs. Thus, when agent n consumes its \\gamma_{nm} are negative which leads to perceived prices \\mu_n higher than trading prices \\lambda_{nm}. When generating perceived prices are lower than trading prices since parameters \\gamma_{nm} would be positive. The total money paid or received by agent n, for real power bilateral trades per time unit, is expressed by \\Lambda_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\lambda_{nm}p_{nm}-\\left(\\underbrace{\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}}_{=\\Gamma_n}\\right), n\\in\\Omega (9); where \\Gamma_n is the part reserved to the system operator."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-settlement","v141-a-M01-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","quote":"After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","quote":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","quote":"Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","quote":"After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","quote":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","quote":"Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e16","quote":"Hence, network charges are provided in a transparent manner before negotiations such that agents can anticipate on the over costs brought by the use of the power system."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","quote":"Both electrical distance and zonal cost allocation policies depend on grid characteristics, supposed time independent. Their unit fees can be adapted to grid’s status (e.g. between day and night)."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","quote":"To define the unit fees, as well as zones, the system operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based on the revenue adequacy and the congestion occurrence rate of the last period."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","quote":"Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e19","quote":"Global stopping criteria associated to (6) are such as \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|r_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}2} and \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|s_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}2} (7)"}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"4c27110fb1b0e571a9eff3dbce41f25e97197fdc82b7c1fb8fcc891547a41f35","applicability":"formalized","reason":"Eq. (6b) supports the bilateral trading-price update statements C001/C002, and Eq. (7)-(8) support the convergence-check statement C006. C003-C005 remain procedural and not_formalized because the supplied evidence states transparency, unit-fee adaptation, and periodic revision but gives no closed-form equation. The B evidence is UNREVIEWED; this output does not claim independent source audit.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001","v141-c-R004-E002"],"rationale":"\\lambda_{nm} is the bilateral price signal updated at the coordination.price_signal node; both frozen demand-to-price and supply-to-price links are the counterproposal channels feeding that update."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-x-p-nm","node_ids":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001","v141-c-R004-E002"],"rationale":"The signed bilateral quantity p_{nm} represents the offered or requested trade quantity carried through the supply and demand links into the bilateral price update."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-rho","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001","v141-c-R004-E002"],"rationale":"The penalty factor \\rho scales the bilateral-quantity mismatch in the price-signal update, so it applies to both demand and supply counterproposal channels."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-pri","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The primal stopping tolerance belongs to the convergence test at coordination.price_signal and is not itself a graph edge relationship."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-dual","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The dual stopping tolerance belongs to the convergence test at coordination.price_signal and is not itself a graph edge relationship."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-gamma-nm","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001","v141-c-R004-E002"],"rationale":"The network charge \\gamma_{nm} is an a priori per-trade price signal applied to bilateral quantities, relevant to both demand and supply links into coordination.price_signal."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","node_ids":["bidding.supply","coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E002"],"rationale":"C001 formalizes the supply-offer contribution to the bilateral trading-price update; the frozen supply-to-price-signal edge is E002."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","node_ids":["bidding.demand","coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001"],"rationale":"C002 formalizes the demand-request contribution to the bilateral trading-price update; the frozen demand-to-price-signal edge is E001."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C006","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001","v141-c-R004-E002"],"rationale":"The stopping test is executed at coordination.price_signal and its residuals aggregate bilateral quantities exchanged through both demand and supply links."}],"revision_requests":["B re-extraction should verify Eq. (7)-(8): the supplied text places squares inside r_n^{k+1} and s_n^{k+1} and also writes \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}2}/\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}2}; C006 preserves that notation, but the exact residual reading remains unreviewed."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
